#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# radicle-seed-prune - automatically detect and prune lower-value repos, spam and abuse from
# a Radicle (heartwood) node's storage. It reclaims disk from stale giants, abandoned repos and
# disposable ones, and it moderates mass-generated spam, media dumps and peers who push their
# own files into repos they are not a delegate of.
#
# DRY-RUN by default: it only prints the plan and changes nothing. Pass --apply to act.
#
# WHAT "PRUNE" MEANS
#   There is no `rad rm`. Unseeding or blocking only changes policy and frees no bytes; only
#   deleting the storage directory does. So a prune is, in this order:
#       rad unseed <rid>   # drop any existing seeding policy
#       rad block  <rid>   # set an explicit block so the default allow-all won't refetch
#       rm -rf storage/<rid>
#   `rad unseed` removes whichever single policy row a repo has, so it must run before
#   `rad block`, or it would wipe the block.
#
# RECOVERABILITY
#   A pruned repo waits QUARANTINE_DAYS in AUDIT_DIR/quarantine, where "quarantine restore
#   <rid>" puts it back. After that it is re-fetchable with `rad unseed <rid>` then `rad seed
#   <rid>`, as long as other nodes still hold it. Both are needed: on a blocked repo, rad seed
#   alone prints success and leaves the block standing. The size/age rules keep a minimum
#   other-seed count for that re-fetch. Rule A's junk-id and rules D, F and H ship with a floor
#   of 0 and may prune the last known copy, which the quarantine covers. Every prune is
#   recorded in an audit log.
#
# SELECTION  (a repo is pruned if it is denied, or if it is NOT excluded AND matches a rule;
# rule G judges peers and prunes nothing)
#
#   Exclusions (never touched by a rule):
#     - pinned repos        (config web.pinned.repositories)
#     - private repos       (rad ls --private, or the identity document says private)
#     - our own repos       (rad ls, or this node has signed refs in the repo)
#     - freshly written     (storage dir mtime within FRESH_GUARD_DAYS; in-flight fetch)
#     - unknown age         (no readable refs. Counted against MAX_SCAN_FAIL_PCT, since a
#                            whole storage of it is a broken scan)
#     - kept                (one rid per line in AUDIT_DIR/keep.txt, written by "quarantine
#                            restore". A did:key: line clears an identity of rule H but
#                            keeps none of its repos)
#     - pardoned            (UNDO_PARDON: a repo known to be wrongly pruned as media, spared
#                            rule F alone until the release named beside it)
#
#   Denied (pruned and blocked on sight, before any rule; pinned, private, own and kept
#   still win, the other exclusions do not):
#     - a repo id in AUDIT_DIR/deny.txt, blocked ahead of time when it is not in storage yet
#     - every repo whose DELEGATES include an identity in deny.txt, which is blocked too. A
#       listed identity's push into someone else's repo does not count, or listing it would
#       condemn any repo it can comment on.
#     - every repo whose delegates' branches and tags, history included, hold copies of the
#       files in AUDIT_DIR/deny-files.tsv: at least COPY_MIN_BYTES, and at least COPY_MIN_PCT%
#       of the bytes there. A file some repo held before every repo it is listed from counts
#       for none. This is the tool's inference from a person's verdict, so the runaway caps
#       and the ratchet count it like a rule's.
#
#   Rule A - junk-named & abandoned, in two branches of differing certainty:
#     A/junk-name: name contains a disposable WORD (test, tmp, old, demo, ...)
#       AND no new commit/COB for > JUNK_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= JUNK_MIN_SEEDS   (default 1: never prune the last copy on a guess)
#       AND its history starts no more than IMPORT_SPARE_DAYS before its `rad init`
#           (a repo whose commit dates do not read counts as not imported, with a WARN)
#     A/junk-id:   the name is NOTHING BUT a random hex id (08a25d0f666d)
#       AND no new commit/COB for > JUNK_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= JUNK_ID_MIN_SEEDS  (default 0: may prune the last copy we know of)
#
#   Rule B - size outlier, stale, replicated:
#       size > ABS_SIZE_FLOOR_MB           (absolute outlier)
#       AND size >= the REL_PCTL-th percentile of all repo sizes  (relative outlier)
#       AND no new commit/COB for > OUTLIER_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= MIN_OTHER_SEEDS
#
#   "Other-seeds" is how many OTHER nodes our routing table says announce the repo. It is no
#   proof a copy exists elsewhere, so a floor of 0 may prune the last copy WE KNOW OF. junk-id,
#   spam-batch, the three media verdicts and malware-op get 0: for machine-generated bulk,
#   media with no project around it, and a malware toolkit, "nobody else seeds it" measures
#   worthlessness. Rules B and C keep a floor, because a big or long-abandoned UNIQUE repo is
#   what a seed exists to preserve.
#
#   Rule C - long-abandoned, replicated (catch-all for whatever the other rules missed):
#       no new commit/COB for > STALE_YEARS_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= MIN_OTHER_SEEDS
#
#   Rules B and C prune only under disk pressure, while free space is under the relaxed
#   threshold (see DISK_AWARE below).
#
#   A SPAM BATCH is a set of repos one script stamped out from a single template: the same name
#   shape with a slot filled in, the same description with a number swapped.
#
#   Rule D - spam batches, decided across the CORPUS:
#       name skeleton contains a random-id slot   (SPAM_REQUIRE_ID=0 accepts any varying slot)
#       AND >= SPAM_MIN_BATCH repos share that name skeleton
#       AND >= SPAM_DESC_AGREE_PCT% of them share ONE description skeleton
#       AND the repo is older than SPAM_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= SPAM_MIN_SEEDS  (default 0: may prune the last copy we know of)
#
#   Names and descriptions are skeletonised (digit runs become #, random-id tokens become %).
#   Both must repeat, because a bulk mirror import shares a name skeleton while each repo
#   carries its own description. Timing is not a signal: a bulk import lands together too, and
#   commit dates are the pusher's to set.
#
#   A MEDIA DUMP is a repo whose tracked files are video, images or audio with no project
#   around them, using the seed as free file hosting.
#
#   Rule F - media dumps, decided by the repo's OWN content:
#       >= MEDIA_MIN_BYTES of media files on the refs this repo's own delegates control
#       AND everything that is NOT media adds up to < MEDIA_TEXT_MAX_BYTES,
#           OR (the ratio path, verdict media-ratio) to < MEDIA_RATIO_TEXT_PER_MILLE/1000 of
#           the images, video and audio at the tips of its branches and tags, which come to
#           >= MEDIA_RATIO_MIN_BYTES, while the repo holds no archive and the tips hold
#           nothing besides the media but <= MEDIA_RATIO_MAX_FILES READMEs
#       AND no source file and no build file (MEDIA_SOURCE_EXTS, MEDIA_BUILD_NAMES)
#       AND the repo is older than MEDIA_STALE_DAYS
#       AND other-seeds >= MEDIA_MIN_SEEDS  (default 0: may prune the last copy we know of)
#     A repo whose name starts like a hostname, holding < MEDIA_HOST_MAX_BYTES of media, is a
#     seed's logo and is spared by every path, unless that hostname ends in a media extension.
#
#   Rule F, batch path - the same dump published again and again, decided by the CORPUS:
#       >= MEDIA_MIN_BYTES of media, of which >= MEDIA_MIN_BYTES sits in blobs that
#         MEDIA_MIN_BATCH or more repos also hold, each with < MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES of
#         anything else, and that this repo was not the first to hold
#       AND everything that is NOT media adds up to < MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES
#       AND no build file; a source file does not spare it
#       AND the same age and seed checks
#
#   Rule G - parasite peers. Judges a PEER and prunes nothing. Blocking one takes --block-peers
#     and an answer, either a y at the prompt or --yes standing in for it:
#       one file of theirs, byte for byte, in >= PARASITE_MIN_REPOS repos they do not delegate
#       AND >= PARASITE_MIN_BYTES of media across those repos
#       AND < PARASITE_TEXT_MAX_BYTES of anything else, anywhere
#       AND they are not a delegate of ANY repo in storage, and are not us
#
#   Rule H - malware operations. Judges an IDENTITY, then single repos. By default it prunes
#     and blocks nothing; the operator adds what it names to the deny list. With
#     MALWARE_PRUNE=1 it prunes every repo a named identity is a delegate of (malware-op), and
#     an --apply run with --block-peers blocks each identity whose repos it prunes. A repo
#     named on its own is only reported. A repo signed by an identity this rule leaves out is
#     spared, and a named delegate of a spared repo is not blocked. Over the names and
#     descriptions of the repos an identity is a delegate of and signed refs in, leaving out
#     identities that are a delegate of a pinned, kept or our own repo, or are in the keep or
#     deny list:
#       one of MALWARE_STRONG_WORDS
#       AND >= MALWARE_MIN_WORDS distinct words of MALWARE_STRONG_WORDS and MALWARE_WEAK_WORDS
#       AND >= MALWARE_MIN_REPOS repos, and >= MALWARE_MIN_SHARE_PCT% of its repos, holding any
#     where a repo holding a word counts only for the identity whose key signed the commit
#     that created it
#     Then one repo the identity path did not name, that is not pinned, private, kept, our
#     own or on the deny list, and none of whose signers that review leaves out:
#       one of MALWARE_STRONG_WORDS in its name or description
#       AND one in a path at a tip of its branches and tags, or in a commit subject on them
#       AND its first commit no more than MALWARE_ORIGINAL_DAYS before its `rad init`
#
#   THE TEXT BUDGET tells a dump from a real repo that carries media: a game or a documented
#   photo archive has a README, licence or build file and clears MEDIA_TEXT_MAX_BYTES many
#   times over. Bytes, not file count, or one 40-byte placeholder would buy an exemption. A
#   source or build file spares a repo from the dump and ratio paths whatever its size. On the
#   batch path, where a README under MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES no longer spares, a build file still
#   does: a small project forked a few times shares its assets, and pruning it costs more than
#   missing a campaign that adds an empty Makefile. A source file does not, because leak
#   campaigns have shipped scripts beside their media.
#
#   FILES ARE JUDGED BY CONTENT. Extensions are only a fast path. A file on neither list has
#   its first 16 bytes matched against media signatures, so renaming a video to .dat does not
#   hide it, and compressed data (a .csv.gz) is judged by what it unpacks to. A file matching
#   nothing counts as text, so an unfamiliar one spares the repo. A known TEXT file over
#   MEDIA_SNIFF_TEXT_BYTES is read too, or a video called README.md would hide itself and blow
#   the budget that spares the repo.
#
#   ONLY THE REPO'S OWN CONTENT COUNTS: the canonical branches and tags, plus the namespaces of
#   the delegates named in refs/rad/id, which the local node writes only after verifying the
#   identity document's signatures. The delegates' issues and comments count, since a dump
#   hides as well in an attachment as in a commit. A COB is read across its whole history,
#   because each op's tree holds only that op's files. That history also reaches other
#   commenters' ops and the commits of any patch a delegate replied to, so only ops a delegate
#   signed count, and openssl checks each signature. Every OTHER peer's namespace is skipped,
#   so pushing a video onto a stranger's near-empty repo cannot put THEIR repo in the plan.
#   Subtracting what other peers hold would fail. Replication mirrors a repo's own branches
#   and COBs into every seeding peer, so it would erase the repo's own README and leave a real
#   project looking like a dump.
#   Left out of rule F: a repo we cannot read; one over MEDIA_MAX_REFS refs or MEDIA_MAX_OPS
#   issue and patch ops, which take minutes to walk; and one whose listing dies part-way, since
#   the fragment reads as less text, and less text condemns. A branch or tag is read at its
#   TIP, so media committed then deleted is missed; walking history would cost far more and
#   count against a repo that dropped a big asset years ago. The walk stops once the repo has
#   more text than any verdict allows.
#
#   A TOKEN README defeats the budget, and below a few MB of media nothing about ONE repo tells
#   it from a small real project. Above that, the ratio path catches a README and nothing else
#   beside a great deal of media; a project carries a licence, a script or a second document.
#   The batch path covers the rest: publishing the same files across many repos is evidence no
#   single repo can manufacture, so there the budget widens to MEDIA_TEXT_CEIL_BYTES. The FIRST
#   holder is left out of its own batch, or reposting somebody's photos into five repos of your
#   own would put their repo in the plan.
#
#   A DUMP NO OTHER NODE SEEDS is pruned like any other. MEDIA_MIN_SEEDS=1 keeps that copy,
#   and rule F then lists what the floor spared, because a silent floor is a standing
#   exemption for anyone who makes sure nobody else seeds their dump.
#
# RULE G JUDGES A PEER and never deletes or prunes. A peer who pushes into repos it is not a
#   delegate of is normally a contributor, so the evidence is ONE FILE, byte for byte, in
#   PARASITE_MIN_REPOS repos where that peer is not a delegate and the repo's own refs do not
#   hold it. Someone attaching screenshots posts a different file each time; one object id in
#   unrelated repos is a campaign. Totals alone are not enough, since someone active across
#   many repos accumulates megabytes honestly.
#
#   A DELEGATE IS NEVER ACCUSED, wherever in storage they are one. The node runs delegate refs
#   through the block list before it checks the signature threshold, so blocking a delegate
#   breaks replication of their repos.
#
#   BLOCKING TAKES TWO OPT-INS. A block is permanent, applies to every repo, and the peer is
#   never told, so --block-peers asks per peer, and refuses when there is no terminal and no
#   --yes. --block-peers --yes blocks every peer the rule names, unattended, and does not imply
#   --apply. The plan prints the exact `rad block` line for every peer named either way, so an
#   operator can act on one peer alone, and each block is audit-logged with its evidence.
#   When it blocks a peer, rad-prune also drops that peer's refs from the repos where it put
#   content of its own. The node filters every namespaced ref it receives through the block
#   list whatever the seeding scope, so those refs do not come back. Dropping them frees no
#   disk until git gc runs, which this tool does not do.
#
#   HOW OLD A REPO IS, for rules D and F, is the older of two dates: the oldest date on any of
#   its refs, and the day this seed first saw it (FIRST_SEEN). Ref dates are the pusher's to
#   set, so a spammer who rewrites every ref looks new; the ledger cannot be reached from
#   outside. Rules A, B and C judge abandonment, so they use activity:
#
#   "Activity" = ANY signed change. Every Radicle interaction (a commit, an issue or patch, a
#   comment, a reaction, an edit, a label) is a git commit under some peer's refs/cobs/* and
#   advances that peer's refs/rad/sigrefs. The tool reads the newest creatordate across ALL
#   refs, every namespace included. creatordate is when the change was authored, so a
#   just-fetched old comment still reads as old.
#
# SAFETY
#   A pruned repo is moved to AUDIT_DIR/quarantine and deleted QUARANTINE_DAYS later. Every
#   repo there was unseeded and blocked before it was moved, so nothing on the node points at
#   the quarantine. rm -rf on it is safe at any moment and costs only the ability to restore.
#   Per-run runaway caps abort a suspiciously large plan unless --force. Deleting a blocked
#   repo's dir with the node running is safe. After a large first run, `sudo systemctl
#   restart radicle-node` (or --restart-node) clears the stale "inventory announce limit" log
#   warning. Some heartwood versions still list removed RIDs in `rad node inventory`
#   afterwards, which is cosmetic.
#
# USAGE
#   Installed on PATH as rad-prune, it runs as `rad prune`, the spelling below. rad-prune and
#   ./rad-prune take the same arguments.
#
#   rad prune                        # preview (dry-run), print the plan
#   rad prune --apply                # apply; asks [y/N] first when run in a terminal
#   rad prune --apply --yes          # answer that prompt with y (scripts / cron)
#   rad prune --apply --force        # apply even if the plan trips the runaway caps
#   rad prune --apply --restart-node # ...and restart the node service afterwards
#   rad prune --block-peers          # block the peers the rules name, asking per peer
#   rad prune --block-peers --yes    # ...without asking (cron)
#
#   rad prune quarantine list             # what a past run pruned and still holds
#   rad prune quarantine restore <rid>    # put it back in storage and re-seed it
#   rad prune quarantine delete <rid>     # or --all: delete now, for good
#   rad prune quarantine purge            # delete whatever is past its window
#   rad prune quarantine files <rid>      # its images, video, audio and archives, as rows
#                                         # to append to deny-files.tsv
#
# --apply scans ONCE, prints the plan, then asks in a terminal before deleting; run
# non-interactively (cron, a pipe) it just applies. Confirming interactively also bypasses the
# runaway caps. --yes (-y) answers every prompt, the per-peer block prompt included:
# --block-peers --yes is how cron blocks the peers rule G finds, and with --apply and
# MALWARE_PRUNE=1 the identities rule H names. --block-peers without --yes and with nobody to
# ask blocks nobody and prints the rad commands instead. --block-peers prunes nothing on its
# own; add --apply.
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#   Everything is configured by environment variable; there are no tuning flags. RAD_HOME is
#   the variable heartwood itself reads:
#
#     RAD_HOME=/var/lib/radicle rad prune              # a seed home that is not ours
#     RAD=/nix/store/.../bin/rad RAD_HOME=/var/lib/radicle rad-prune
#     JOBS=4 OUTLIER_STALE_DAYS=120 rad prune          # any tunable in the config block
#
#   RAD (default: rad from PATH) and RAD_HOME (default: `rad path`, else ~/.radicle) locate the
#   node; STORAGE, CONFIG and AUDIT_DIR derive from RAD_HOME. Most thresholds in the config
#   block are overridable the same way; a few that need the implementation to pick are plain
#   constants.
#
# Run as the user that owns the Radicle home (the seed account), or point RAD_HOME at it.
# Cron-safe: dry-run by default, deterministic, and self-logging.
#
